        "text": "void showPublic() // Public member function\n\n{\n\ncout<<\"X= \"<<X;\n\n}:\n\nint main()\n\n(\n\nPublicTest PT1; //PT1 is an object of class PublicTest PT1.X; //access private data members\n\nPT1.showPublic(); // access public member function getch();\n\nreturn 0;\n\n}\n\nIn the above program, the class Public Test is defined which comprise of member X and one member function showPublic() in its public part. The data m\n\nNOT\n\nCALE\n\ne dan\n\nmber1\n\nis accessed within the member function showPublic() and outside the class, i.e. in main() function, without generating any error message. Similarly, the member function showPublic() is also accessed in main() function.\n\nIf no access specifier is mentioned in a class for a member then it will combined as\n\nprivate by default.\n\nThe following program demonstrates private and public access specifier.\n\n#include<iostream.h>\n\n#include<conio.h>\n\n{\n\nclass Access\n\nint A; // private by default\n\nvoid GetA() // private by default\n\n}\n\ncout<<\"I am private member accessible only through public member function\"<<endl;\n\npublic:\n\nint B; // public\n\nvoid GetB()\n\n{\n\nGetA();\n\ncout<<\"I am B in public\"<<endl;\n\n}\n\nint main()\n\nAccess cAccess; //cAccess is an object of the class Access\n\nComputer Science-XII\n\n//cAccess.A = 2; // WRONG because A is private data member\n\n//cAccess.GetA(); //WRONG because GetA() is private member function cAccess.B=10; // Okay because B is public data member\n\ncAccess.GetB(); // Okay because GetB() is public member function\n\ngetch():\n\n}\n\nreturn 0;\n\nThe above program explains the scope (or accessibility) of the private and public da members and member functions in detail. Also, comments are added with each line code in main() program for the explanation of its accessibility level.\n\nThe output of the program is:\n\nI am private member accessible only through public member function\n\nI am B in public\n\n8.1.4 Data hiding\n\nData Hiding is an important concept of C++ programming language in which the membe of a class are protected against illegal access from outside the class. In C languag struct is used but it cannot hide data of its data members. Whereas in C++, we got facility of hiding data using different access levels: private, protected, public in classes.\n\nPrivate data members and member functions can only be accessed by the members the same class defining them and cannot be accessed from outside the class. Simila protected members (data members and member functions) of a class can be access only by the class defining them and its derived classes. By using friend function, private and protected members (data members and member functions) of a class also be accessed. Except these, private and protected members are not access\n\nConsider the following simple program to explain the concept of constructor in a class.\n\n#include <iostream.h>\n\n#include <conio.h>\n\nclass ConstTest\n\n{\n\npublic:\n\nConstTest() // Constructor\n\n{\n\ncout<<< \"I am Constructor\";\n\n}\n\n}:\n\nint main()\n\nConstTest CT;// CT is an object to the class ConstTest getch();\n\nreturn 0;\n\n}\n\nOutput of the program\n\nI am Constructor\n\nIn the above example, a class ConstTest is defined which have the constructi ConstTest() in its public part. In main() program, there is no explicit call to this construc and it is automatically called when the object CT of this class is created. It should b noted that Constructors are always called at the moment when objects are created. They\n\ncannot be called explicitly as member functions are called.\n\nNOT-FOR-SALE\n\n{\n\n(\n\ni. User-defined constructory Explicit constructor\n\nWhen users define constructors in class for their own purpose, especially for initialization of variables, then such types of constructors are called user defined constructors. When constructor is declared for a class, the compiler no longer provides an implicit default constructor. So, the objects should be declared according to the constructor prototypes that have been defined for the class.\n\n// user-defined (parameterized) constructor example\n\n#include <iostream.h>\n\n#include <conio.h> .\n\nclass CExample\n\npublic:\n\nint a,b,c;\n\nm\n\nCExample (int n, int m)\n\na=n;\n\nb=m;\n\nint multiply ().\n\n{\n\nc=a*b;\n\nreturn c;\n\n}\n\nint main()\n\nCExample CObj (50,10):\n\nint result=CObj.multiply();\n\ncout<<\"Multiplication Results is: \"<<<result;\n\ngetch();\n\nreturn 0;\n\n}\n\nOutput of the program\n\nMultiplication Result is: 500\n\nHere, a constructor CExample with two parameters of type int has been declared that initializes private variables a and b. When the object declaration is done as follows:\n\nCExample CObj (5,10);\n\nThe following declaration is not correct, because, we have declared the class to have a explicit constructor (user-defined constructor) to which the values should be passed.\n\nCExample CObj:\n\nA user-defined constructor can be either parameterized or non-parameterized.\n\nparameterized constructor is the one which accepts arguments while the object declared and nonparameterized constructor is the one whose object declaration is don without specifying arguments. The program given above defines parameterize constructor. The following program explains non-parameterized constructor.\n\n#include <iostream.h>\n\n#include <conio.h>\n\nclass CNonParameter\n\npublic:\n\nCNonParameter()\n\ncout<<\"I am non-parameterized constructor\";\n\n}\n\nNOT PA\n\n}\n\n{\n\nint main()\n\nCNonParameter NP: getch():\n\nreturn 0;\n\n}\n\nUnit-8 Objects and Classes\n\nOutput of the program\n\nI am non-parameterized constructor",
